<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The one colour the system draws on the app's behalf.

    Android tints a notification's small icon and its app-name line with
    `NotificationCompat.Builder.setColor`, and with nothing supplied it picks a
    neutral grey — so every Aevistle notification looked like a system message
    rather than like this app. `#0B6BD8` is the `accent-azure-l` token from
    `src/styles/theme.css`, which is the app's own default accent in light
    mode, so the shade and the running app agree without a second value to keep
    in step by hand.

    (Written without the token's leading dashes on purpose: an XML comment may
    not contain a double hyphen, and the build fails on it rather than warning.)

    Deliberately the light-mode azure rather than the dark one: the shade
    renders this against both backgrounds and the darker of the two is the one
    that stays legible on white.
-->
<resources>
    <color name="notification_accent">#0B6BD8</color>
</resources>
